  <div class="callout warn"><strong>No current GovWait estimate.</strong> INZ’s current processing-time tool does not provide a 50% or 80% figure for this closed route. GovWait does not copy the timing of another resident category or turn old programme totals into a personal forecast.</div>

  <h2>What the official record says</h2>
  <p>INZ describes the 2021 Resident Visa as a one-off pathway created for eligible migrants affected by COVID-19 disruption. Applications opened on December 1, 2021 and closed on July 31, 2022. The route was tied to historical eligibility and presence requirements; it is not a visa that a new applicant can choose today.</p>
  <p>In an update published June 13, 2024, INZ reported receiving <strong>106,349 applications covering 217,586 people</strong>. It reported <strong>103,961 applications approved, covering 212,649 people</strong>. Those are dated programme totals, not a present-day queue estimate.</p>

  <h2>Why the totals do not reveal your remaining wait</h2>
  <p>Subtracting approvals from applications does not produce a reliable “applications still pending” number. The public totals on that page do not split every non-approved application into pending, declined, withdrawn or otherwise closed outcomes. They also do not provide submission dates, complexity or verification status for an individual file.</p>
  <p>INZ says decision time depends on the visa, the applicant’s circumstances and the information supplied. It may need more information or third-party verification. A programme-wide total therefore cannot be converted into a date for one application.</p>

  <h2>How an existing applicant can check progress</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Use the online account connected to the application.</strong> INZ says the account shows whether it needs more information and provides available progress information.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the contact details used in the application.</strong> INZ may phone, email or post a notification in the account if it needs supporting information.</li>
    <li><strong>Use INZ’s official contact route when appropriate.</strong> Its status guidance says an applicant can contact INZ if an application is taking longer than the normal timeframe for that visa.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>GovWait cannot access an application, identify its queue position or predict its decision. Do not post application numbers, passport details or other personal information here.</p>

  <h2>Do not substitute another resident-visa time</h2>
  <p>The current <a href="/new-zealand/permanent-resident-visa/">Permanent Resident Visa</a>, Skilled Migrant Category and other residence routes have different eligibility rules and processing cohorts. Their published 50% and 80% figures do not describe an old 2021 Resident Visa file.</p>
  <p>For current routes, read <a href="/guides/how-new-zealand-visa-processing-times-work/">how INZ processing times work</a> and use the <a href="/new-zealand/">New Zealand processing-time index</a>. GovWait keeps working days, source dates and percentile meanings intact rather than converting them into a guessed calendar deadline.</p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <h3>Can I apply for the 2021 Resident Visa now?</h3>
  <p>No. INZ’s current visa page marks the route closed to new applicants on July 31, 2022.</p>
  <h3>What is the current average processing time?</h3>
  <p>INZ’s current processing-time tool does not provide a current 50% or 80% figure for this route. An old forum timeline or another visa category is not a valid replacement.</p>
  <h3>Do the June 2024 totals show how many cases are pending?</h3>
  <p>No. The difference between applications received and approved is not labeled as a pending backlog on the official update, so GovWait does not describe it that way.</p>

